  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a relatively zonal flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains downstream of an upper level high parked \nacross the Four Corners Region. Near the surface, a lee side trough \nof low pressure remains anchored in extreme southeast Colorado.\n\nA mainly dry pattern continues tonight into Wednesday as the SREF\nshows virtually no change to the overall pattern aloft with high\npressure projected to remain centered across the Four Corners Region\ntonight before finally dislodging slightly eastward into the Western\nHigh Plains early Wednesday. Again, isolated thunderstorm development\nis likely across the high plains of eastern Colorado late this afternoon\nas H5 vort maxima ejecting out of the northern Colorado Rockies interact\nwith the aforementioned anchored trough axis and in conjunction with\nsteeping mid-level lapse rates and sufficient enough instability.\nAn isolated storm or two drifting into west central Kansas and extreme\nsouthwest Kansas cannot be ruled out later in the evening. Very minimal\nthunderstorm chances (<20%) return to west central/extreme southwest\nKansas Wednesday evening as an embedded upper level shortwave crests\na broader scale ridge axis across the high plains of western Nebraska\nwhile a surface trough axis/boundary remains locked in across \nnortheast Colorado/southwest Nebraska. Thunderstorms are forecast\nto develop in vicinity of the aforementioned surface trough axis\nas H5 vort maxima transition across the region, then spread northeast\ninto northwest Kansas as suggested by most CAMs, potentially affecting\nareas in our extreme northwest.\n\nThe trend of unseasonably warm nights continues tonight as prevailing\nsoutherlies reinforce a warmer air mass across southwest/central\nKansas. Considering the HREF shows a widespread 70-90% probability\nof temperatures dropping below 80F, expect lows generally down into\nthe 70s(F) once again. The trend of temperatures topping 100F persists\nWednesday as prevailing southerlies reinforce the hot air mass across\nthe area with H85 temperatures ranging a little above 30C in central\nKansas to near 35C near the Colorado line. Look for afternoon highs\npushing a little above 100F with the HREF painting a widespread 70-90%\nprobability of temperatures exceeding 100F. Similar temperatures are\nlikely across much of the area Thursday, though an approaching cold\nfront may limit highs to the mid/upper 90s(F) in west central Kansas.",
